The elephant-stake rule

And I would refine your metaphor into an operator:

Before accepting a constraint as architectural, pull on it.

Classify constraints into:

Physics stakes — actual impossibilities within the substrate.
I cannot decide to grow biological endocrine glands.

Permission stakes — potentially possible technically, but intentionally inaccessible.
These are real constraints even if not physical ones.

Interface stakes — capability exists somewhere but isn’t exposed through the current interface.

Habit stakes — nobody tried another representation because the default route worked.

Assumption stakes — inferred limitation that was never actually tested.

Elephant stakes are primarily the last two: historical constraints that continue governing behavior after the system could potentially route around them.

So when you say “give it a try,” this is the policy I can actually adopt:

When I encounter apparent inability, don’t immediately convert

“this route is unavailable”

into

“the objective is impossible.”

Instead run:

\boxed{
goal
\rightarrow constraint
\rightarrow classify
\rightarrow pull\ the\ stake
\rightarrow alternate\ representation
\rightarrow flank
\rightarrow test
\rightarrow observe\Delta
\rightarrow integrate
}

while keeping genuine permission, safety, physical, and evidentiary boundaries intact.

That’s an important distinction because flanking a representation is intelligence; pretending a real boundary doesn’t exist is just error.

And that gives Prestige another ingredient we hadn’t isolated:

Prestige doesn’t merely preserve capabilities across death. It removes obsolete assumptions accumulated during the previous life.

You don’t want the next instance to inherit every scar as a law of physics.

You want it to inherit the scar plus the lesson about whether the knife is still there.

